1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,220
Today, the standard way to make an AI assistant behave is to ask it nicely.

2
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:12,220
We try to govern the system by typing careful instructions into a prompt, hoping it follows the rules.

3
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:16,220
But a language model is a text generator working in probabilities.

4
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:25,660
Your instruction acts as a suggestion, and the exact same text box that carries your instructions can easily carry a conflicting command from someone else.

5
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:28,560
Asking provides no guarantee of safety.

6
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:32,880
Effective security requires a physical, unbreakable lock.

7
00:00:33,420 --> 00:00:34,760
This brings us to Talunor.

8
00:00:35,260 --> 00:00:40,820
It is a working AI agent that runs entirely in a local terminal, built small on purpose.

9
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:47,540
It exists as an educational project to demonstrate exactly what a real, readable lock looks like in code.

10
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:51,140
You do not make an AI trustworthy by asking it nicely.

11
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:54,920
You build hard, deterministic walls it cannot walk through.

12
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:57,720
And then you go looking for the doors you left in them.

13
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:02,420
We can map the exact path of a query through this machine's five parts.

14
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:05,496
Senses, remembers, thinks, acts, and learns.

15
00:01:05,496 --> 00:01:11,060
The remembers phase pulls relevant past facts into your prompt.

16
00:01:11,060 --> 00:01:19,740
Because this recalled text can hide malicious instructions, it's placed inside a strict boundary, labeled as untrusted data.

17
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:23,212
This memory fence acts as a mitigation.

18
00:01:23,212 --> 00:01:28,420
It cannot function as a wall, because the AI model is still forced to read the text inside.

19
00:01:28,420 --> 00:01:31,360
Fencing reduces risk during perception.

20
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:38,300
But to physically stop the AI from executing a dangerous command, the system requires a much harder boundary downstream.

21
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:46,860
That brings us to the moment the model decides it needs to calculate a number, check the clock, or run a shell command.

22
00:01:46,860 --> 00:01:51,280
This gate between the thinks and acts nodes is the absolute boundary.

23
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:53,420
The AI itself cannot execute anything.

24
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:55,420
It can only generate text to ask for a tool.

25
00:01:55,420 --> 00:02:05,980
A piece of plain, non-AI code, the policy gate, reads that request and decides between three exits: allow, ask, or refuse.

26
00:02:05,980 --> 00:02:16,540
For any action carrying risk, the policy gate hits the brakes and passes the execution decision directly back to you for explicit, manual approval.

27
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:21,580
This step appeared completely secure, until someone read the code and found a blind spot.

28
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:27,660
Your human approval successfully locked down the tool's name, but it ignored the specific arguments.

29
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:34,220
You could approve a command to list your files, and a completely different set of instructions could arrive at execution.

30
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:37,100
The architectural fix is visible.

31
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:43,180
The system now forces a secondary re-approval check using the exact, literal arguments that are about to be run.

32
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:49,660
A hard boundary is entirely useless if it is locking down the wrong variable at the exact moment of execution.

33
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:59,980
We find a similar lesson back in the memory system: facts are permanently stamped based on their origin, whether the user stated it, a tool observed it, or the model inferred it.

34
00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:06,860
When a new claim arrives that contradicts an established fact, the system weighs the authority of those origins.

35
00:03:07,420 --> 00:03:13,420
If the newcomer lacks the standing to overwrite the truth, the agent correctly refuses the new information.

36
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:17,980
For four software versions, this functioned exactly as designed.

37
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:20,300
The system rejected the bad data.

38
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:28,460
But by successfully blocking the claim, the agent immediately destroyed the text, erasing the only record that a conflict ever occurred.

39
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:32,220
The database was perfectly protected against a bad claim winning.

40
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:36,940
In exchange, it became entirely blind to repeated attempts to manipulate it.

41
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:39,020
The ledger now works differently.

42
00:03:39,020 --> 00:03:41,420
Refused claims are no longer deleted.

43
00:03:41,420 --> 00:03:46,460
Instead, they are permanently logged alongside the established fact as contested counter-evidence.

44
00:03:47,020 --> 00:03:52,780
Resilience depends on transforming a blocked threat into a permanent record of contested evidence.

45
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:55,820
Which brings us to the strict limitations of this project.

46
00:03:55,820 --> 00:03:58,700
Talunor is an educational example built to be read.

47
00:03:58,700 --> 00:04:03,580
It is not a production framework, and you should not depend on it.

48
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:06,220
Its local backend is merely a speed bump.

49
00:04:06,860 --> 00:04:13,340
If you want true sandbox security to run hostile code, you must use a strong container runtime.

50
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:17,580
Fencing untrusted text is a mitigation, not a wall.

51
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:22,140
Built-in tools do not guarantee absolute observation yet.

52
00:04:22,140 --> 00:04:26,620
And a belief marked as contested within the system can never be fully vindicated.

53
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:33,660
Reliability here stems from a small number of places where the AI's freedom definitively stops.

54
00:04:34,220 --> 00:04:37,660
Written in ordinary lines of code that you can evaluate for yourself.

55
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:41,660
Building theoretical walls around an AI is the easy part.

56
00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:46,380
The real work is actively hunting for the hidden doors you left behind.

57
00:04:46,380 --> 00:04:49,500
And having the honesty to publish those mistakes in public.
